## Phase 1: The Journalist
### Chapter 3: Knowing and Knowing Well

Chapter 3 explained why research ethics matters and how researchers should protect the people whose information or behavior they study. This chapter made me think more carefully about what makes research ethical. Before reading, I mostly thought research ethics meant completing forms and getting approval before collecting data. However, the historical examples show why so many protections are necessary. The Tuskegee syphilis study, Milgram's obedience experiments, and Humphrey's tearoom study all produced knowledge, but they also harmed, decieved, or violated the privacy of participants. These example demonstrate that valuable results and good intentions are not enough to make a study ethical. 

This chapter connected to my reasearch project idea about how social media platforms track users interests and use their previous activity to decide what content appears in their feeds. The Facebook emotional-contagion study especially caught my attention because it shows that algorithms do more than simply organize content. By showing some users fewer positive or negative posts, Facebook influenced the emotional tone of what those users later posted. This raised questions about what modern recommendation systems might be doing everyday without users realizing it. Facebook argued that its terms of service allowed this research, but I do not think accepting a long agreement is the same as knowingly volenteering for psychological experiment. 

The Belmount Report's principles of respect for persons, beneficence, and justice provide a useful way to judge situations like this. Reasearcher must respect peoples ability to make their own choices, minimize possible harm, and distribute the risks and benefits of research fairly. These responsibilities also apply when researchers study public online data. Although a post may be publicly accessible, a creator may not expect it to be collected, analyzed, or repbulished for data research. 

The biggest question I had after reading this chapter was whether information being public authomatically makes it ethical to study. I think researchers need to consider context, privavy expectations, and possible consequences instead of relying only on whether they can technically access the data.
